From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kristof Imeri <kristof@lanth.me>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 05:48:18 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 52/54] compositor: outline every floating window and shadow
 those that draw none

Windows used to blend into whatever was behind them, most of all dark
windows over other dark windows, and only Wayland windows that drew no
shadow of their own got an outline. Like on Windows 11, give every
floating normal window, dialog and utility window a thin neutral line
along the inside of its frame's rounded edge that lightens dark windows
and darkens light ones, whether it is a Wayland or an X11 window, draws
its own title bar and shadow or has Kestrel's. Popups, menus, tooltips,
override-redirect and shaped X11 windows, the desktop, and maximized,
tiled and fullscreen windows go without.

Windows whose buffer doesn't reach past their frame, where a client
would draw a shadow of its own, also get a shadow from the shadow
factory, the same one for Wayland and X11 windows, in place of the
shadow Mutter gave opaque undecorated X11 windows. It is never painted
under the window, so translucent and blurred parts of it stay clear,
and it reaches undecorated translucent X11 windows such as Electron's,
which had none. The default shadows grow a little softer and wider for
every window.

The line is as many whole device pixels wide as fit in a logical pixel,
so it stays crisp at fractional scales, and its outer edge is
antialiased exactly like the rounded corners' clip, so it meets the
window's edge without a gap or halo. It is drawn in
bands along the edges, so it costs no full-size pass. Window actors
drop their shadows when they're unrealized, since the shadows' textures
belong to the graphics context that a GPU reset replaces, and the
shadow factory shares one shadow between every window of the same
shape. A window's paint volume is recomputed when it gains or loses
focus, since its shadow changes size.

Upstream-Status: luft-only Kestrel's window shadows and outline
